		{
			"id": "117440297649073161",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@rauschma/117440297572072416",
			"author": {
				"name": "Axel Rauschmayer",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@rauschma",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/291/906/591/238/009/original/76fd351b6f7a848e.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2026-10-14T16:52:08.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I recently read something about “open loops” (=unfinished tasks) being a cognitive burden and am wondering:</p><p>What if it were easy to add older open tabs to an archive of links (and close them)? Data per link:<br>• URL<br>• Title<br>• Topic (crucial for retrieval—think one or more short tags such as “JS”, “politics”, “cooking”, etc.)<br>• If available: date of the tab being opened<br>• Maybe: Date of last update of web page</p><p>Users would have to be prompted for a tag—unless a tab is in a tab group.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "117440312468904476",
			"parentId": "117440287856934473",
